se_duration('6:30') \u2192 390.0. parse_duration('1:00') \u2192 60.0. parse_duration('10:00') \u2192 600.0.\n\nNow, the course URL. Let me wait for VALID_URL. \n\nAlso \u2014 one more consideration: `LinuxAcademyIE()` instantiation. InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None). Fine.\n\nAlso, `LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 classmethod using `_get_class` ... `ie_key` returns cls.ie_key() \u2192 cls._get... Actually:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        return cls.__name__\n```\n\nHmm, in youtube_dl it's:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls._get_class().__name__)\n```\n\nHmm, something like that. Returns 'LinuxAcademyIE'? Wait \u2014 in youtube-dl, `ie_key` returns the class name without 'IE'? Let me check: `get_info_extractor` uses `gen_extractor_classes()`... The `ie_key` in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls.__name__)\n```\n\nHmm, actually in youtube_dl/extractor/common.py:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls._get_class().__name__)\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me just check via get_info('InfoExtractor.ie_key'). Or simpler: in the test, assert `entry['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 comparing to the same source. But better to hardcode 'LinuxAcademy'. In youtube-dl, InfoExtractor has:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls.__name__)\n```\n\nHmm, but for YoutubeIE, ie_key is 'Youtube'... In youtube_dl, the class is `YoutubeIE` and ie_key returns... Let me think. `YoutubeIE.ie_key()` \u2192 'Youtube'. Because:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls._get_class().__name__[:-2])\n```\n\nHmm, no. Actually in youtube_dl/extractor/common.py:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls.__name__)\n```\n\nand YoutubeIE defines `_IE_NAME = 'youtube'`... but ie_key is used for `get_info_extractor`... In youtube_dl, `YoutubeIE.ie_key()` returns 'Youtube'. Let me verify: in youtube_dl/extractor/common.py (youtube-dl):\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls.__name__)\n```\n\nHmm, that would give 'YoutubeIE'. But `get_info_extractor('Youtube')`... The gen_extractor_classes returns classes; InfoExtractorsDict... In youtube_dl/extractor/__init__.py:\n\n```python\ndef gen_extractor_classes():\n    \"\"\"Return a list of supported extractors.\"\"\"\n    return _BUILD_EXTRACTORS...\n```\n\nHmm. Actually I recall in youtube-dl:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls.__name__)\n```\n\nNo wait. Let me look at actual youtube-dl common.py:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def ie_key(cls):\n        \"\"\"A string for getting the InfoExtractor with get_info_extractor\"\"\"\n        return compat_str(cls._get_class().__name__)\n```\n\nI'm going in circles. The safest: assert `entry['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()`. That's a valid postcondition (the entry references this IE by key). I can additionally assert it equals 'LinuxAcademy' if I confirm. Let me query get_info('InfoExtractor.ie_key') too.\n\nOK let me now make the tool calls. Batch:\n\n1. get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._VALID_URL')\n2. get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._real_extract')\n3. get_info('InfoExtractor._download_webpage')\n4. get_info('InfoExtractor._parse_json')\n5. get_info('InfoExtractor._search_regex')\n6. get_info('InfoExtractor._extract_m3u8_formats')\n7. get_info('InfoExtractor._sort_formats')\n8. get_info('InfoExtractor.ie_key')\n9. get_info('unified_timestamp')\n10. get_info('parse_duration')\n11. get_info('clean_html')\n12. get_info('urljoin')\n\nThat's 12 calls in one block. Fine.\n\nAfter that, write the final test script.\n\nLet me draft the final test script now, with placeholders for URL patterns:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\nfrom __future__ import unicode_literals, absolute_import\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import parse_duration, unified_timestamp\n\n\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, m3u8_formats=None):\n    \"\"\"Build a LinuxAcademyIE with the network layer patched out.\"\"\"\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\n        ie, '_download_webpage',\n        lambda url, video_id, *args, **kwargs: webpage)\n    if m3u8_formats is not None:\n        monkeypatch.setattr(\n            ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats',\n            lambda *args, **kwargs: m3u8_formats)\n    return ie\n\n\nclass TestCoursePath(object):\n\n    COURSE_URL = '...'  # per VALID_URL\n\n    def test_full_course_playlist(self, monkeypatch):\n        module = {...}\n        webpage = 'window.module = %s;' % json.dumps(module)\n        ie = _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage)\n        result = ie._real_extract(self.COURSE_URL)\n        assert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\n        ...\n```\n\nWait \u2014 json.dumps produces double quotes; the regex `({.+?})\\s*;` non-greedy: for nested JSON, `.+?` will stop at the first `}` that is followed by `\\s*;`. Since the JSON ends with `}` followed by `;`, and inner `}` are followed by `,` or other chars, the non-greedy match will extend to the final `}`. But careful: if an inner object is the last thing before the closing brace, e.g. `{\"a\": {\"b\": 1}};` \u2014 the regex `({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 re.search scans from the earliest start. Starting at `{` of the outer object: `.+?` minimal expansion until `}` followed by `;`. The first `}` followed by `;` is the last one. Inner `}` (after 1) is followed by `}` not `;`, so no match there; regex backtracks and expands. So the full outer object matches. \n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety: re.search tries the earliest starting position. The pattern starts with `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*` so it anchors at 'window.module'. Then `({.+?})\\s*;`. Since `.` doesn't match newline (no DOTALL), everything must be on one line. json.dumps produces single-line output by default. \n\nBut also: json.dumps with non-ASCII? I'll use ASCII strings only, or ensure_ascii default True. Fine.\n\nOne more subtlety: the module JSON contains `items` with dicts; all on one line \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, `_parse_json` \u2014 youtube_dl's `_parse_json(json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False)`:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False):\n        try:\n            return json.loads(json_string, ...)\n```\n\nActually it uses `json.loads` with a custom hook? In youtube_dl:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False):\n        try:\n            if transform_source:\n                json_string = transform_source(json_string)\n            return json.loads(json_string, cls=... )\n```\n\nHmm, youtube_dl's version:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False):\n        try:\n            return json.loads(\n                json_string, object_pairs_hook=..., \n```\n\nI think it's just json.loads with optional transform. Either way, valid JSON works.\n\nNow the single-video webpage:\n