ect is the last thing before the closing brace, e.g. `{\"a\": {\"b\": 1}};` \u2014 the regex `({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 re.search scans from the earliest start. Starting at `{` of the outer object: `.+?` minimal expansion until `}` followed by `;`. The first `}` followed by `;` is the last one. Inner `}` (after 1) is followed by `}` not `;`, so no match there; regex backtracks and expands. So the full outer object matches. \n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety: re.search tries the earliest starting position. The pattern starts with `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*` so it anchors at 'window.module'. Then `({.+?})\\s*;`. Since `.` doesn't match newline (no DOTALL), everything must be on one line. json.dumps produces single-line output by default. \n\nBut also: json.dumps with non-ASCII? I'll use ASCII strings only, or ensure_ascii default True. Fine.\n\nOne more subtlety: the module JSON contains `items` with dicts; all on one line \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, `_parse_json` \u2014 youtube_dl's `_parse_json(json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False)`:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False):\n        try:\n            return json.loads(json_string, ...)\n```\n\nActually it uses `json.loads` with a custom hook? In youtube_dl:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False):\n        try:\n            if transform_source:\n                json_string = transform_source(json_string)\n            return json.loads(json_string, cls=... )\n```\n\nHmm, youtube_dl's version:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, ignore_extra=False):\n        try:\n            return json.loads(\n                json_string, object_pairs_hook=..., \n```\n\nI think it's just json.loads with optional transform. Either way, valid JSON works.\n\nNow the single-video webpage:\n\n