rams.get('prefer_free_formats')` \u2014 inside `_formats_key`, in the branch `if f.get('vcodec') == 'none'` or else branch: `if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')`. So with a fake downloader with `params = {}`, `.get` returns None \u2192 falsy \u2192 fine.\n\nAlso `_parse_json` with fatal=False calls `self.report_warning` \u2014 which uses `self._downloader`? Let me check `report_warning`:\n\n```python\ndef report_warning(self, msg, video_id=None):\n    ...\n    if self._downloader:\n        self._downloader.to_screen(...)\n```\n\nHmm, in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\n    def report_warning(self, msg, video_id=None):\n        '''\n        Report a warning during information extraction\n        '''\n        msg = ('WARNING:' + ... )\n        ...\n        self.to_screen(msg)\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\n    def report_warning(self, msg, video_id=None):\n        _msg = '%s: %s' % (video_id or ..., msg)\n        ...\n        if self._downloader:\n            self._downloader.report_warning(msg)\n        else:\n            ...\n```\n\nI don't remember exactly. In our tests, `_parse_json(..., fatal=False)` will only hit the warning path if JSON parsing fails. We'll provide valid JSON, so no warning. But the lesson search with default='{}' \u2192 `_parse_json('{}', item_id, fatal=False)` \u2192 json.loads('{}') \u2192 {} \u2192 fine, no warning.\n\nBut wait \u2014 in test 4/5 (no lesson), `_search_regex` with tuple patterns and default='{}': if neither `window.lesson` nor `player.lesson` matches \u2192 returns '{}'. Good, no warning path.\n\nHmm, but actually wait: in `_search_regex`, the first line: `if not self._downloader.params.get('no_color') and compat_os_name != 'nt' and sys.stderr.isatty():`. This is evaluated ALWAYS (before checking mobj). So every `_search_regex` call needs `self._downloader` to be a valid object with `params`. So the fake downloader is required for all tests.\n\nAlso `sys.stderr.isatty()` \u2014 under pytest, stderr might be captured; isatty() returns False typically, so short-circuit: `compat_os_name != 'nt'` is True on Linux, then `sys.stderr.isatty()` \u2014 pytest's capture replaces sys.stderr with something that has isatty() \u2192 False. OK fine. But to be safe, the fake downloader just needs `params` dict.\n\nLet me define:\n\n```python\nclass _FakeDownloader(object):\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n```\n\nAnd instantiate `LinuxAcademyIE(_FakeDownloader())`.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double-check InfoExtractor.__init__ signature in this codebase. Let me query get_info('InfoExtractor.__init__'). Also check `report_warning` in case. And `to_screen`. Let me also check `LinuxAcademyIE` full class to see if there's a `_login` or other attributes (the get_info for _real_extract showed the class has `...` before _real_extract \u2014 there might be `_LOGIN_URL`, `_NETRC_MACHINE`, `_login` etc., but _real_extract doesn't call them, so irrelevant).\n\nAlso `ie_key()` returns `cls.__name__[:-2]` \u2192 'LinuxAcademy'. So `entry['ie_key'] == 'LinuxAcademy'`. \n\nNow `_extract_m3u8_formats` \u2014 I'll mock it entirely (it calls `_download_webpage_handle` which would hit network). Mock returns fake formats.\n\n`_sort_formats` \u2014 real one works with fake formats as long as `self._downloader.params` exists. With format `{'url': ..., 'format_id': 'hls-1', 'ext': 'mp4'}`: `_formats_key`: f.get('ext') = 'mp4' present. field_preference None \u2192 preference = f.get('preference') \u2192 None \u2192 0; ext 'mp4' not in ['f4f','f4m'] \u2192 0. protocol = f.get('protocol') or determine_protocol(f) \u2014 determine_protocol(f) uses f.get('url')... determine_protocol:\n\n```python\ndef determine_protocol(f):\n    protocol = f.get('protocol')\n    if not protocol:\n        url = f['url']\n        protocol = 'm3u8' if determine_ext(url) == 'm3u8' else ...\n```\n\nHmm, determine_protocol might need 'url'. Our format has 'url'. protocol would be 'https' \u2192 proto_preference 0. vcodec not 'none' \u2192 else branch: acodec check \u2192 f.get('acodec') is None \u2260 'none' \u2192 skip. `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 ORDER = ['webm', 'flv', 'mp4']; ext_preference = ORDER.index('mp4') = 2. Fine. So real `_sort_formats` works with a single format. \n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety: `_sort_formats` first line: `if not formats: raise ExtractorError`. We have one format. Fine.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 `_formats_key` has `from ..utils import determine_ext` inside \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso `determine_protocol` \u2014 let me make sure it doesn't need more. It's:\n\n```python\ndef determine_protocol(f):\n    protocol = f.get('protocol')\n    if not protocol:\n        url = f['url']\n        protocol = 'm3u8' if determine_ext(url) == 'm3u8' else 'f4m' if determine_ext(url) == 'f4m' else compat_urllib_parse.urlparse(url).scheme\n    ...\n```\n\nSomething like that. With url 'https://...' \u2192 'https'. Fine.\n\nTo be safe, I'll give the fake format: `{'url': 'https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8', 'format_id': 'hls-1', 'ext': 'mp4', 'protocol': 'm3u8_native'}`. With protocol set, determine_protocol not needed... wait, `protocol = f.get('protocol') or determine_protocol(f)` \u2014 'm3u8_native' \u2192 truthy \u2192 proto_preference: `0 if protocol in ['http', 'https'] else -0.5 if protocol == 'rtsp' else -0.1` \u2192 -0.1. Fine.\n\nOK. Now the URLs:\n\n- Course: `https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/1234` \u2192 course_id='1234'. item_id='1234'.\n- Lesson: `https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/1498/lesson/5993` \u2192 chapter_id='1498', lesson_id='5993' \u2192 item_id='1498-5993'.\n\nWait, check the regex: `courses/lesson/course/(?P<chapter_id>\\d+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>\\d+)`. So URL `https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/1498/lesson/5993`. Yes.\n\nurljoin for lesson items: base = 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/1234', path '/lesson/lesson-1' \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/lesson-1'. And relative path 'lesson/lesson-2' \u2192 urljoin(base, 'lesson/lesson-2') \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/lesson/lesson-2'. Let me use absolute URL for the second lesson to keep it simple: 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2' \u2014 urljoin returns path as-is since it matches `^(?:[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9+-.]*:)?//`. Good.\n\nNow, unified_timestamp('2019-06-25 12:00:00'): Let me trace. date_formats(day_first) \u2014 in youtube_dl/utils.py:\n\n```python\ndef date_formats(day_first=True):\n    return [\n        # YYYY-MM-DD\n        '%Y-%m-%d',\n        ...\n    ]\n```\n\nHmm, actually unified_timestamp's date_formats include things like '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'? Let me think. youtube_dl utils has:\n\n```python\n_DATE_FORMATS = (\n    '%d %B %Y',\n    '%d %b %Y',\n    ...\n)\n_DATE_FORMATS_DAY_FIRST = ...\n_DATE_FORMATS_MONTH_FIRST = ...\n```\n\nAnd unified_timestamp:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n\n    date_str = re.sub(r'[,|]', '', date_str)\n    pm_delta = 12 if re.search(r'(?i)PM', date_str) else 0\n    timezone, date_str = extract_timezone(date_str)\n    date_str = re.sub(r'(?i)\\s*(?:AM|PM)(?:\\s+[A-Z]+)?', '', date_str)\n    m = re.search(r'\\d{1,2}:\\d{1,2}(?:\\.\\d+)?(?P<tz>\\s*[A-Z]+)$', date_str)\n    if m:\n        date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group('tz'))]\n    m = re.search(...)\n    ...\n    for expression in date_formats(day_first):\n        try:\n            dt = datetime.datetime.strptime(date_str, expression) - timezone + datetime.timedelta(hours=pm_delta)\n            return calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())\n        except ValueError:\n            pass\n    ...\n```\n\nAnd date_formats:\n\n```python\ndef date_formats(day_first=True):\n    return [\n        # YYYY-MM-DD\n        '%Y-%m-%d',\n        '%Y/%m/%d',\n        ...\n        # Complete dates\n        '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S',  # ?\n    ]\n```\n\nHmm. Actually youtube_dl's date_formats:\n\n```python\ndef date_formats(day_first=True):\n    return [\n        # YYYY-MM-DD\n        r'%Y-%m-%d',\n        ...\n    ]\n```\n\nI don't remember precisely. The safest approach: in the test, compute `expected = unified_timestamp('2019-06-25 12:00:00')` and assert `entry['timestamp'] == expected` plus `expected is not None`. This verifies the plumbing without depending on my memory of date formats. If unified_timestamp can't parse it, expected is None and the assertion `expected is not None` fails \u2014 then I'd know to change the format. But I can't iterate interactively... well, I can reason.\n\nLet me think harder about youtube_dl's unified_timestamp date formats. From youtube_dl/utils.py (actual source):\n\n```python\n_DATE_FORMATS = (\n    ('%d %B %Y', 'dayfirst'),\n    ...\n)\n```\n\nHmm no. Actual youtube-dl utils.py:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n\n    date_str = re.sub(r'(?i)[,\\s]+', ' ', date_str)  # not sure\n    ...\n```\n\nOK let me just recall the real code:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n\n    date_str = re.sub(r'[,|]', '', date_str)\n    pm_delta = 12 if re.search(r'(?i)PM', date_str) else 0\n    timezone, date_str = extract_timezone(date_str)\n    date_str = re.sub(r'(?i)\\s*(?:AM|PM)(?:\\s+[A-Z]+)?', '', date_str)\n    m = re.search(\n        r'\\d{1,2}:\\d{1,2}(?:\\.\\d+)?(?P<tz>\\s*[A-Z]+)$', date_str)\n    if m:\n        date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group('tz'))]\n    m = re.search(\n        r'^([0-9]{4,}-[0-9]{1,2}-[0-9]{1,2}T[0-9]{1,2}:[0-9]{1,2}:[0-9]{1,2}\\.[0-9]{6})[0-9]+$',\n        date_str)\n    if m:\n        date_str = m.group(1)\n\n    for expression in date_formats(day_first):\n        try:\n            dt = datetime.datetime.strptime(date_str, expression) - timezone + datetime.timedelta(hours=pm_delta)\n            return calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())\n        except ValueError:\n            pass\n    timetuple = email.utils.parsedate_tz(date_str)\n    if timetuple:\n        return calendar.timegm(timetuple) + pm_delta * 3600\n```\n\nAnd date_formats:\n\n```python\ndef date_formats(day_first=True):\n    return [\n        # YYYY-MM-DD\n        '%Y-%m-%d',\n        '%Y/%m/%d',\n        ...\n    ]\n```\n\nHmm, the actual youtube-dl date_formats:\n\n```python\ndef date_formats(day_first=True):\n    return [\n        # YYYY-MM-DD\n        '%Y-%m-%d',\n        '%Y/%m/%d',\n        # DD/MM/YYYY\n        '%d/%m/%Y' if day_first else '%m/%d/%Y',\n        ...\n    ]\n```\n\nNo... The real one:\n\n```python\n_DATE_FORMATS = (\n    '%d %B %Y',\n    '%d %b %Y',\n    '%B %d %Y',\n    '%b %d %Y',\n    '%B %d %Y',\n    ...\n)\n```\n\nArgh. OK, the actual youtube-dl utils.py has:\n\n```python\ndef date_formats(day_first=True):\n    return [\n        # YYYY-MM-DD\n        '%Y-%m-%d',\n        '%Y/%m/%d',\n        # MM/DD/YYYY and DD/MM/YYYY\n        '%d/%m/%Y' if day_first else '%m/%d/%Y',\n        '%m/%d/%Y' if day_first else '%d/%m/%Y',\n        # YYYY-MM-DD hh:mm\n        ...\n    ]\n```\n\nI genuinely don't remember. Let me use get_info('date_formats') to check! That's the right move.\n\nAlso get_info('extract_timezone') maybe. And 'InfoExtractor.__init__' and 'report_warning' to confirm the downloader requirement.\n\nActually, simpler and fully robust: use ISO-ish '2019-06-25T12:00:00' or just use a date-only string '2019-06-25' \u2014 '%Y-%m-%d' is definitely in date_formats (it's the most basic). For time, I could use '2019-06-25 12:00:00' \u2014 need to confirm format exists. Let me query date_formats.\n\nAlternatively, avoid the issue: use date-only strings '2019-06-25' and '2019-06-26' for timestamps. unified_timestamp('2019-06-25') \u2192 parses with '%Y-%m-%d' \u2192 calendar.timegm \u2192 1561420800. I computed earlier: 2019-06-25 00:00:00 UTC = 1561420800. Let me double check: 2019-01-01 00:00:00 UTC = 1546300800 (known). 2019 is not a leap year. Jan 31, Feb 28, Mar 31, Apr 30, May 31 \u2192 150 days to Jun 1? Jan 1 + 31 = Feb 1 (31 days), +28 = Mar 1 (59), +31 = Apr 1 (90), +30 = May 1 (120), +31 = Jun 1 (151). So Jun 1 is day 152 of year, 151 days after Jan 1. Jun 25 = 151 + 24 = 175 days after Jan 1. 175 * 86400 = 15,120,000. 1546300800 + 15120000 = 1,561,420,800. Yes, 2019-06-25 00:00:00 UTC = 1561420800.\n\nBut rather than hardcode, I'll assert equality with unified_timestamp('2019-06-25') computed in the test \u2014 and also assert it's not None. That's robust regardless of my arithmetic.\n\nHmm, but is that a meaningful assertion? Yes: it verifies the extractor passes the date string through unified_timestamp. And `is not None` guards against the format not being supported (in which case the test would fail loudly rather than trivially pass with None == None). Good.\n\nActually, let me also query date_formats to pick a format confidently. I'll do one more round of get_info calls:\n\n- 'date_formats'\n- 'InfoExtractor.__init__'\n- 'InfoExtractor.report_warning' (to check downloader usage in _parse_json fatal=False path \u2014 actually not needed since we won't hit it)\n- 'extract_timezone' (to make sure '2019-06-25 12:00:00' doesn't do something weird \u2014 extract_timezone looks for timezone patterns like 'UTC', 'GMT', '+0000'; plain string \u2192 returns timezone as datetime.timedelta(0)? Let me check: extract_timezone returns (tz, date_str) where tz is a timedelta. For no timezone found, returns (datetime.timedelta(0), date_str)? I think it handles 'UTC'/'GMT' etc. and returns zero delta otherwise.)\n\nLet me batch: 'date_formats', 'InfoExtractor.__init__', 'extract_timezone', 'determine_protocol' (for _sort_formats safety), 'InfoExtractor.report_warning'.\n\nActually report_warning \u2014 in _parse_json fatal=False path only on JSON error; we won't hit it. Skip. But wait \u2014 there's another place: `_search_regex` when no match and default is NO_DEFAULT and fatal=True \u2192 raises RegexNotFoundError. In title fallback, if no pattern matches \u2192 raise. We always provide a match. OK.\n\nHmm, one more: `_search_regex` line `if not self._downloader.params.get('no_color') and compat_os_name != 'nt' and sys.stderr.isatty():` \u2014 needs `self._downloader.params`. With fake downloader params={} \u2192 .get('no_color') \u2192 None \u2192 `not None` \u2192 True \u2192 then `compat_os_name != 'nt'` \u2192 on Linux True \u2192 `sys.stderr.isatty()` \u2192 under pytest capture, sys.stderr is replaced by pytest's EncodedFile/CaptureIO which has isatty() \u2192 False. So _name = name. Fine. On Windows, compat_os_name == 'nt' \u2192 short-circuit. Fine either way.\n\nNow `InfoExtractor.__init__` \u2014 let me confirm it accepts downloader positionally. Standard:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, downloader=None):\n    self._downloader = downloader\n```\n\nI'll query to be sure.\n\nNow let me also double check `_parse_json` \u2014 it's `json.loads(json_string)`. Our JSON strings are valid. Good.\n\nNow, the course webpage. The regex: `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;`. I'll build:\n\n```python\nmodule_json = json.dumps(module)\nwebpage = '<script>window.module = %s;</script>' % module_json\n```\n\njson.dumps output has no newlines. Good. But careful: json.dumps of strings containing '</script>'? Not an issue for regex.\n\nWait, one potential issue: `.+?` non-greedy with the JSON containing `;`? If any string value contains ';', the regex could match early? The pattern is `({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 it needs `}` immediately before optional whitespace and `;`. A `;` inside a string value would only terminate the match if preceded by `}`. E.g. `{\"a\":\"x;\",\"b\":{}}` \u2014 hmm, `{\"a\":\"x;\"` \u2014 after `;` comes `\",...` not end. The regex requires `}\\s*;`. In `{\"a\":\"x;\",\"b\":{}};` \u2014 is there a `}` followed by `;` before the end? The `}}` at the end: first `}` (inner) followed by `}` \u2014 no. Second `}` followed by `;` \u2014 yes. So match is the whole thing. But what about `{\"a\":\"x;}\",\"b\":1};`? The `}` inside the string followed by `\"` \u2014 no. OK. To be safe, avoid `;` and `}` inside string values. My values are simple. Good.\n\nAlso avoid newlines in JSON \u2014 json.dumps default has none. Good.\n\nNow the module for the course test:\n\n```python\nmodule = {\n    'title': 'Linux Academy Course',\n    'md_desc': 'Course markdown description',\n    'desc': '<b>Course plain description</b>',\n    'duration': '1:00',\n    'items': [\n        'not-a-dict',                                   # line 172-173\n        {},                                             # no 'type' -> skip (185-186)\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'Section', 'slug': 'section'},\n            'course_name': 'Chapter One',\n            'course_module': 'module-1',\n        },                                              # 179-182, chapter_number 1\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'Quiz', 'slug': 'quiz'},\n        },                                              # 185-186\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n            'url': None,\n        },                                              # 187-189\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'Lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n            'url': '/lesson/lesson-1',\n            'title': 'Lesson One',\n            'md_desc': 'Lesson one md description',\n            'date': '2019-06-25',\n            'duration': '5:00',\n        },                                              # 190-202 entry 1\n        {\n            'type': {'slug': 'lesson'},                 # no 'name' -> type_field('name') -> ''\n            'url': 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2',\n            'lesson_name': 'Lesson Two',\n            'description': '<i>Lesson two description</i>',\n            'created_on': '2019-06-26',\n            'duration': '6:30',\n        },                                              # entry 2 (title fallback, desc via clean_html, ts via created_on)\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'section'},                # no 'slug'\n            'course_name': 'Chapter Two',\n            'course_module': 'module-2',\n        },                                              # chapter_number 2 (increment branch)\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'LESSON', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n            'url': 'lesson/lesson-3',\n            'title': None,\n            'lesson_name': 'Lesson Three',\n            'text': '<p>Lesson three text</p>',\n            'duration': 'not-a-duration',\n        },                                              # entry 3: chapter Two/2, desc via clean_html(text), ts None, duration None\n    ],\n}\n```\n\nWait, entry 3: url 'lesson/lesson-3' relative \u2192 urljoin(base, 'lesson/lesson-3') \u2192 base is 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/1234' \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/lesson/lesson-3'. Good \u2014 covers relative-without-slash urljoin branch.\n\n'title': None \u2192 `item.get('title') or item.get('lesson_name')` \u2192 'Lesson Three'. description: md_desc missing \u2192 None; clean_html(item.get('description')) \u2192 clean_html(None) \u2192 None (clean_html returns None for None); then clean_html(item.get('text')) \u2192 'Lesson three text'. timestamp: date missing \u2192 unified_timestamp(None) \u2192 None; created_on missing \u2192 None \u2192 None. duration: parse_duration('not-a-duration') \u2192 None. Good \u2014 covers parse_duration None branch too.\n\nHmm wait, 'title': None \u2014 json.dumps(None) \u2192 'null' \u2192 item.get('title') \u2192 None. Good.\n\nAlso for entry 2, timestamp: date missing \u2192 unified_timestamp(None) \u2192 None \u2192 then unified_timestamp('2019-06-26'). Good \u2014 covers the `or` second branch.\n\nExpected entries:\n\nentry1:\n- _type 'url_transparent'\n- url 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/lesson-1'\n- ie_key 'LinuxAcademy'\n- title 'Lesson One'\n- description 'Lesson one md description'\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-25')\n- duration 300.0\n- chapter 'Chapter One'\n- chapter_id 'module-1'\n- chapter_number 1\n\nentry2:\n- url 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2'\n- title 'Lesson Two'\n- description 'Lesson two description'\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-26')\n- duration 390.0\n- chapter 'Chapter One'\n- chapter_id 'module-1'\n- chapter_number 1\n\nentry3:\n- url 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/lesson/lesson-3'\n- title 'Lesson Three'\n- description 'Lesson three text'\n- timestamp None\n- duration None\n- chapter 'Chapter Two'\n- chapter_id 'module-2'\n- chapter_number 2\n\nplaylist:\n- _type 'playlist'\n- id '1234'\n- title 'Linux Academy Course'\n- description 'Course markdown description'\n- duration 60.0\n- len(entries) == 3\n\nAlso assert that non-lesson/section items didn't produce entries (len check covers).\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, one thing about `type_field` for the `{}` item: `try_get(item, lambda x: x['type'][key], compat_str)` \u2014 item is {} \u2192 item['type'] \u2192 KeyError \u2192 try_get returns None \u2192 ''. type_fields = ('','') \u2192 no 'section', no 'lesson' \u2192 continue at 185-186. Good.\n\nAnd for the 'not-a-dict' string item \u2014 line 172-173. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 does `try_get` catch KeyError? youtube_dl's try_get:\n\n```python\ndef try_get(src, getter, expected_type=None):\n    try:\n        v = getter(src)\n    except (AttributeError, KeyError, TypeError, IndexError):\n        return None\n    if expected_type is not None and not isinstance(v, expected_type):\n        v = None\n    return v\n```\n\nHmm, actually youtube_dl's try_get:\n\n```python\ndef try_get(src, getter, expected_type=None):\n    if not isinstance(getter, (list, tuple)):\n        getter = [getter]\n    for g in getter:\n        try:\n            v = g(src)\n        except (AttributeError, KeyError, TypeError, IndexError):\n            pass\n        else:\n            if expected_type is None or isinstance(v, expected_type):\n                return v\n    return None\n```\n\nSomething like that. Either way, KeyError caught. Good.\n\nNow the single-video tests.\n\nTest 2 (window.lesson, full info):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8\"}];'\n    'window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Intro Lesson\", \"md_desc\": \"Lesson md description\", '\n    '\"date\": \"2019-06-25\", \"duration\": \"10:00\"};'\n)\n```\n\ninfo:\n- id '1498-5993'\n- formats == fake formats (sorted)\n- title 'Intro Lesson'\n- description 'Lesson md description'\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-25')\n- duration 600.0\n\nAlso assert m3u8_url captured == 'https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8' and video_id passed == '1498-5993'.\n\nTest 3 (player.lesson alternative + desc/created_on fallbacks):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video2.m3u8\"}];'\n    'player.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Second Lesson\", \"desc\": \"<b>Second lesson description</b>\", '\n    '\"created_on\": \"2019-06-26\"};'\n)\n```\n\ninfo:\n- title 'Second Lesson'\n- description 'Second lesson description' (clean_html)\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-26')\n- duration None (parse_duration(None) \u2192 None)\n\nTest 4 (no lesson \u2192 title via '>Lecture :'):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video3.m3u8\"}];'\n    '<h3>Lecture: My Lecture Title</h3>'\n)\n```\n\ninfo:\n- title 'My Lecture Title'\n- 'description' not in info, 'timestamp' not in info, 'duration' not in info\n- id '1498-5993'\n\nWait \u2014 the regex `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` on '<h3>Lecture: My Lecture Title</h3>' \u2192 matches '>Lecture: My Lecture Title' \u2192 value 'My Lecture Title'. Good.\n\nTest 5 (no lesson \u2192 title via lessonName):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video4.m3u8\"}];'\n    \"var lessonName = 'Fallback Lesso