So match is the whole thing. But what about `{\"a\":\"x;}\",\"b\":1};`? The `}` inside the string followed by `\"` \u2014 no. OK. To be safe, avoid `;` and `}` inside string values. My values are simple. Good.\n\nAlso avoid newlines in JSON \u2014 json.dumps default has none. Good.\n\nNow the module for the course test:\n\n```python\nmodule = {\n    'title': 'Linux Academy Course',\n    'md_desc': 'Course markdown description',\n    'desc': '<b>Course plain description</b>',\n    'duration': '1:00',\n    'items': [\n        'not-a-dict',                                   # line 172-173\n        {},                                             # no 'type' -> skip (185-186)\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'Section', 'slug': 'section'},\n            'course_name': 'Chapter One',\n            'course_module': 'module-1',\n        },                                              # 179-182, chapter_number 1\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'Quiz', 'slug': 'quiz'},\n        },                                              # 185-186\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n            'url': None,\n        },                                              # 187-189\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'Lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n            'url': '/lesson/lesson-1',\n            'title': 'Lesson One',\n            'md_desc': 'Lesson one md description',\n            'date': '2019-06-25',\n            'duration': '5:00',\n        },                                              # 190-202 entry 1\n        {\n            'type': {'slug': 'lesson'},                 # no 'name' -> type_field('name') -> ''\n            'url': 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2',\n            'lesson_name': 'Lesson Two',\n            'description': '<i>Lesson two description</i>',\n            'created_on': '2019-06-26',\n            'duration': '6:30',\n        },                                              # entry 2 (title fallback, desc via clean_html, ts via created_on)\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'section'},                # no 'slug'\n            'course_name': 'Chapter Two',\n            'course_module': 'module-2',\n        },                                              # chapter_number 2 (increment branch)\n        {\n            'type': {'name': 'LESSON', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n            'url': 'lesson/lesson-3',\n            'title': None,\n            'lesson_name': 'Lesson Three',\n            'text': '<p>Lesson three text</p>',\n            'duration': 'not-a-duration',\n        },                                              # entry 3: chapter Two/2, desc via clean_html(text), ts None, duration None\n    ],\n}\n```\n\nWait, entry 3: url 'lesson/lesson-3' relative \u2192 urljoin(base, 'lesson/lesson-3') \u2192 base is 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/1234' \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/lesson/lesson-3'. Good \u2014 covers relative-without-slash urljoin branch.\n\n'title': None \u2192 `item.get('title') or item.get('lesson_name')` \u2192 'Lesson Three'. description: md_desc missing \u2192 None; clean_html(item.get('description')) \u2192 clean_html(None) \u2192 None (clean_html returns None for None); then clean_html(item.get('text')) \u2192 'Lesson three text'. timestamp: date missing \u2192 unified_timestamp(None) \u2192 None; created_on missing \u2192 None \u2192 None. duration: parse_duration('not-a-duration') \u2192 None. Good \u2014 covers parse_duration None branch too.\n\nHmm wait, 'title': None \u2014 json.dumps(None) \u2192 'null' \u2192 item.get('title') \u2192 None. Good.\n\nAlso for entry 2, timestamp: date missing \u2192 unified_timestamp(None) \u2192 None \u2192 then unified_timestamp('2019-06-26'). Good \u2014 covers the `or` second branch.\n\nExpected entries:\n\nentry1:\n- _type 'url_transparent'\n- url 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/lesson-1'\n- ie_key 'LinuxAcademy'\n- title 'Lesson One'\n- description 'Lesson one md description'\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-25')\n- duration 300.0\n- chapter 'Chapter One'\n- chapter_id 'module-1'\n- chapter_number 1\n\nentry2:\n- url 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2'\n- title 'Lesson Two'\n- description 'Lesson two description'\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-26')\n- duration 390.0\n- chapter 'Chapter One'\n- chapter_id 'module-1'\n- chapter_number 1\n\nentry3:\n- url 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/lesson/lesson-3'\n- title 'Lesson Three'\n- description 'Lesson three text'\n- timestamp None\n- duration None\n- chapter 'Chapter Two'\n- chapter_id 'module-2'\n- chapter_number 2\n\nplaylist:\n- _type 'playlist'\n- id '1234'\n- title 'Linux Academy Course'\n- description 'Course markdown description'\n- duration 60.0\n- len(entries) == 3\n\nAlso assert that non-lesson/section items didn't produce entries (len check covers).\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, one thing about `type_field` for the `{}` item: `try_get(item, lambda x: x['type'][key], compat_str)` \u2014 item is {} \u2192 item['type'] \u2192 KeyError \u2192 try_get returns None \u2192 ''. type_fields = ('','') \u2192 no 'section', no 'lesson' \u2192 continue at 185-186. Good.\n\nAnd for the 'not-a-dict' string item \u2014 line 172-173. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 does `try_get` catch KeyError? youtube_dl's try_get:\n\n```python\ndef try_get(src, getter, expected_type=None):\n    try:\n        v = getter(src)\n    except (AttributeError, KeyError, TypeError, IndexError):\n        return None\n    if expected_type is not None and not isinstance(v, expected_type):\n        v = None\n    return v\n```\n\nHmm, actually youtube_dl's try_get:\n\n```python\ndef try_get(src, getter, expected_type=None):\n    if not isinstance(getter, (list, tuple)):\n        getter = [getter]\n    for g in getter:\n        try:\n            v = g(src)\n        except (AttributeError, KeyError, TypeError, IndexError):\n            pass\n        else:\n            if expected_type is None or isinstance(v, expected_type):\n                return v\n    return None\n```\n\nSomething like that. Either way, KeyError caught. Good.\n\nNow the single-video tests.\n\nTest 2 (window.lesson, full info):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8\"}];'\n    'window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Intro Lesson\", \"md_desc\": \"Lesson md description\", '\n    '\"date\": \"2019-06-25\", \"duration\": \"10:00\"};'\n)\n```\n\ninfo:\n- id '1498-5993'\n- formats == fake formats (sorted)\n- title 'Intro Lesson'\n- description 'Lesson md description'\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-25')\n- duration 600.0\n\nAlso assert m3u8_url captured == 'https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8' and video_id passed == '1498-5993'.\n\nTest 3 (player.lesson alternative + desc/created_on fallbacks):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video2.m3u8\"}];'\n    'player.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Second Lesson\", \"desc\": \"<b>Second lesson description</b>\", '\n    '\"created_on\": \"2019-06-26\"};'\n)\n```\n\ninfo:\n- title 'Second Lesson'\n- description 'Second lesson description' (clean_html)\n- timestamp unified_timestamp('2019-06-26')\n- duration None (parse_duration(None) \u2192 None)\n\nTest 4 (no lesson \u2192 title via '>Lecture :'):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video3.m3u8\"}];'\n    '<h3>Lecture: My Lecture Title</h3>'\n)\n```\n\ninfo:\n- title 'My Lecture Title'\n- 'description' not in info, 'timestamp' not in info, 'duration' not in info\n- id '1498-5993'\n\nWait \u2014 the regex `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` on '<h3>Lecture: My Lecture Title</h3>' \u2192 matches '>Lecture: My Lecture Title' \u2192 value 'My Lecture Title'. Good.\n\nTest 5 (no lesson \u2192 title via lessonName):\n\n```python\nwebpage = (\n    'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video4.m3u8\"}];'\n    \"var lessonName = 'Fallback Lesson Name';\"\n)\n```\n\nRegex: `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(?P<value>(?:(?!\\1).)+)\\1` \u2014 matches `lessonName = 'Fallback Lesson Name'` \u2192 group 1 = \"'\", value = 'Fallback Lesson Name'. \n\nNote: `_search_regex` with group='value' returns mobj.group('value'). Good.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 in test 4 and 5, the lesson search: patterns ('window\\.lesson...', 'player\\.lesson...') \u2014 neither matches \u2192 default='{}' \u2192 _parse_json('{}', item_id, fatal=False) \u2192 {} \u2192 falsy \u2192 skip update. Then title fallback. Good \u2014 covers default='{}' path and `if lesson:` False branch.\n\nIn test 2/3, `if lesson:` True branch covered.\n\nNow \u2014 one concern: in test 4/5, `_search_regex` for 'lesson' with default='{}': the code path `elif default is not NO_DEFAULT: return default`. Good.\n\nNow the fake downloader. Let me define:\n\n```python\nclass _FakeDownloader(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal stand-in for YoutubeDL: only what InfoExtractor touches.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n```\n\nAnd `ie = LinuxAcademyIE(_FakeDownloader())`.\n\nWait \u2014 does InfoExtractor.__init__ signature = (self, downloader=None)? Let me confirm via get_info('InfoExtractor.__init__'). I'll include it in the next batch.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, `_search_regex` uses `sys.stderr.isatty()`. Under pytest with capture, sys.stderr is pytest's capture object which has isatty(). If run with `-s`, sys.stderr is the real stderr \u2014 has isatty(). Fine.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtle issue: `if not self._downloader.params.get('no_color') and compat_os_name != 'nt' and sys.stderr.isatty():` \u2014 if isatty() is True (running in a terminal without capture), _name gets ANSI codes \u2014 harmless.\n\nNow `_sort_formats` \u2014 real, with fake downloader params={}. `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` \u2192 None. Fine.\n\nLet me now also double-check `determine_protocol` isn't needed: I'll set 'protocol': 'm3u8_native' in the fake format. Actually wait \u2014 `_formats_key`: `protocol = f.get('protocol') or determine_protocol(f)` \u2192 'm3u8_native' truthy \u2192 skip. Then `proto_preference = 0 if protocol in ['http', 'https'] else -0.5 if protocol == 'rtsp' else -0.1` \u2192 -0.1. Then vcodec: f.get('vcodec') \u2192 None \u2260 'none' \u2192 else branch: `if f.get('acodec') == 'none'` \u2192 None \u2260 'none' \u2192 skip. `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` \u2192 None \u2192 ORDER = ['webm', 'flv', 'mp4'] \u2192 ext_preference = ORDER.index('mp4') = 2. Fine. Sort works.\n\nActually, hmm \u2014 wait. Let me re-read `_sort_formats` from the tool output:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats, field_preference=None):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found')\n    for f in formats:\n        if 'tbr' not in f and f.get('abr') is not None and (f.get('vbr') is not None):\n            f['tbr'] = f['abr'] + f['vbr']\n\n    def _formats_key(f):\n        from ..utils import determine_ext\n        if not f.get('ext') and 'url' in f:\n            f['ext'] = determine_ext(f['url'])\n        if isinstance(field_preference, (list, tuple)):\n            ...\n        preference = f.get('preference')\n        if preference is None:\n            preference = 0\n            if f.get('ext') in ['f4f', 'f4m']:\n                preference -= 0.5\n        protocol = f.get('protocol') or determine_protocol(f)\n        ...\n```\n\nOK so with my format dict it works. Good \u2014 I'll use the real `_sort_formats`.\n\nHmm, but `determine_protocol` import \u2014 it's imported at top of common.py per the snippet (`from youtube_dl.utils import determine_ext, determine_protocol, ExtractorError`). Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 the mock for `_extract_m3u8_formats`. Signature called: `self._extract_m3u8_formats(m3u8_url, item_id, 'mp4', entry_protocol='m3u8_native', m3u8_id='hls')`. My fake:\n\n```python\ndef fake_extract_m3u8(m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n    captured['m3u8_url'] = m3u8_url\n    captured['video_id'] = video_id\n    captured['ext'] = ext\n    captured['kwargs'] = kwargs\n    return [dict(fake_format)]\n```\n\nAnd assert captured['m3u8_url'] == 'https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8', captured['video_id'] == '1498-5993', captured['ext'] == 'mp4', kwargs == {'entry_protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'm3u8_id': 'hls'}.\n\nAnd assert result['formats'] is the returned list (identity or equality). After _sort_formats, the list is sorted (single element, unchanged) and 'ext' may be set. Assert equality of relevant fields.\n\nNow, `_download_webpage` mock: called as `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)`. Fake: `lambda url, video_id, *args, **kwargs: webpage`. Also capture url to assert the extractor passes the original URL:\n\n```python\ndef fake_download(url_or_request, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n    captured['url'] = url_or_request\n    captured['video_id'] = video_id\n    return webpage\n```\n\nAssert captured['url'] == course_url and captured['video_id'] == '1234' (item_id).\n\nNow \u2014 the fixture approach. I'll write a helper that builds the IE with monkeypatch:\n\n```python\ndef _build_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, m3u8_formats=None):\n    captured = {}\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE(_FakeDownloader())\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(url_or_request, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        captured['download_url'] = url_or_request\n        captured['download_video_id'] = video_id\n        return webpage\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n\n    if m3u8_formats is not None:\n        def fake_extract_m3u8(m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n            captured['m3u8_url'] = m3u8_url\n            captured['m3u8_video_id'] = video_id\n            captured['m3u8_ext'] = ext\n            captured['m3u8_kwargs'] = kwargs\n            return m3u8_formats\n\n        monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract_m3u8)\n\n    return ie, captured\n```\n\nmonkeypatch.setattr on instance: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)` \u2014 this sets instance attribute; on teardown it restores (deletes the instance attr since the original was on the class... actually monkeypatch records the original attribute: for setattr on an instance where the attribute comes from the class, monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) will do `setattr(obj, name, value)` and on undo `delattr(obj, name)`? Let me think: pytest's monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value) \u2014 it uses `setattr` and records the old value via `getattr(target, name)`... Actually monkeypatch stores (target, name, old_value) and on undo does setattr(target, name, old_value) \u2014 which would set the instance attribute to the bound method. Hmm, that leaves an instance attribute pointing to the bound method \u2014 functionally identical, and the object is discarded anyway. No state pollution since `ie` is local. Fine.\n\nActually, monkeypatch.setattr has special handling: if the attribute wasn't in the instance `__dict__`, it uses `delattr` on undo? Let me recall pytest's MonkeyPatch.setattr:\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(...)\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nand undo:\n\n```python\ndef undo(self):\n    for obj, name, value in reversed(self._setattr):\n        if value is not notset:\n            setattr(obj, name, value)\n        else:\n            delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nSo for an instance, oldval = getattr(ie, '_download_webpage') \u2192 the bound method \u2192 undo does setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', bound_method) \u2192 sets instance attr. Since ie is local to the test, no pollution. Good.\n\nAlternatively, patch the class: `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', ...)` \u2014 but then the fake receives self. Instance-level patching is cleaner. Keep instance-level.\n\nNow \u2014 timestamps. Let me confirm date_formats supports '2019-06-25'. I'll query 'date_formats'. If '%Y-%m-%d' is there (it certainly is \u2014 it's the most common), fine.\n\nLet me also double check `unified_timestamp('2019-06-25')`: extract_timezone(date_str) \u2014 looks for timezone markers; none \u2192 returns (timedelta(0)?, date_str). Hmm, extract_timezone:\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(r'(?i)(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})$', date_str)\n    ...\n    m = re.search(r'(?i)(?P<tz>{tz})$'.format(tz='|'.join(...)), date_str)\n    ...\n    timezone = datetime.timedelta(...)\n    ...\n    return timezone, date_str\n```\n\nFor no match... does it return (datetime.timedelta(), date_str)? I believe there's a fallback returning zero timezone. Let me query 'extract_timezone' to be safe. Actually, unified_timestamp is used everywhere in youtube-dl with plain dates like '2019-06-25' and works. I'm confident. But the assertion `expected is not None` will catch any surprise... except if it fails, my test fails. Let me just query date_formats and extract_timezone to be sure.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, one more consideration for `unified_timestamp`: `date_str = re.sub('[,|]', '', date_str)` \u2014 wait, the tool output shows `re.sub('[,|]', '', date_str)` \u2014 that's a character class [,|] removing commas and pipes. '2019-06-25' unaffected. Good.\n\nNow, next batch of get_info calls:\n- 'date_formats'\n- 'extract_timezone'\n- 'InfoExtractor.__init__'\n- 'determine_protocol' (optional, for _sort_formats confidence)\n- 'try_get' (confirm exception catching)\n- 'InfoExtractor.report_warning' (not needed... skip)\n- 'LinuxAcademyIE' (full class \u2014 to see if there are other attributes like _LOGIN_REQUIRED that _real_extract might touch... the _real_extract source shown doesn't. Skip? Actually let me get the full class to be thorough \u2014 it showed \"...\" before _real_extract, meaning there are other members (like _VALID_URL, maybe _login). Not needed for the test.)\n\nLet me do: 'date_formats', 'extract_timezone', 'InfoExtractor.__init__', 'try_get', 'determine_protocol'.\n\nThen write the final test.\n\nActually also \u2014 one more check: `LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2192 `cls.__name__[:-2]` \u2192 'LinuxAcademy'. Confirmed from tool output. \n\nNow let me also think about whether `LinuxAcademyIE(_FakeDownloader())` works \u2014 InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None). Yes.\n\nOne more potential gotcha: `_real_extract` is called directly; it doesn't call `self._match_id` or `suitable()`. Good.\n\nAlso \u2014 the `re` module import at top of the extractor module \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, about coverage of line 176's lambda: `lambda x: x['type'][key]` \u2014 covered by all items with 'type'.\n\nLine 182: `chapter_number = 1 if not chapter_number else chapter_number + 1` \u2014 both branches covered (first section \u2192 1; second section \u2192 2).\n\nLine 198: `unified_timestamp(item.get('date')) or unified_timestamp(item.get('created_on'))` \u2014 both branches covered (entry1 date; entry2 created_on; entry3 both None).\n\nLine 191: `item.get('md_desc') or clean_html(item.get('description')) or clean_html(item.get('text'))` \u2014 entry1 md_desc; entry2 description; entry3 text. All three branches. \n\nLine 190: `item.get('title') or item.get('lesson_name')` \u2014 entry1 title; entry3 lesson_name (title None). Both branches.\n\nLine 209: `module.get('md_desc') or clean_html(module.get('desc'))` \u2014 md_desc present \u2192 first branch. To cover the second branch (clean_html(module.get('desc'))), I'd need a module without md_desc. Hmm \u2014 should I add another course test without md_desc? That would cover the `or` fallback. Let me add a small second course test: module with no md_desc, desc='<b>Fallback course description</b>', items=[] (empty list \u2014 covers empty entries too). Then description == 'Fallback course description', entries == [].\n\nWait \u2014 with items=[], the for loop doesn't execute... but lines 171-202 are already covered by test 1. The empty-items test covers line 209's second branch and empty entries. Good, cheap test.\n\nHmm, but actually \u2014 is `clean_html(module.get('desc'))` reached when md_desc is None? `module.get('md_desc')` \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 evaluate `clean_html(module.get('desc'))` \u2192 clean_html('<b>Fallback course description</b>') \u2192 'Fallback course description'. Yes.\n\nAlso for the playlist title: module.get('title') \u2014 in test 1 present. Could also test missing title \u2192 None. Not needed for coverage (line 208 executes regardless).\n\nSimilarly line 234: `lesson.get('md_desc') or clean_html(lesson.get('desc'))` \u2014 test 2 md_desc, test 3 desc. Both covered.\nLine 235: date / created_on \u2014 test 2 date, test 3 created_on. Both covered.\nLine 233: lesson_name \u2014 both tests.\nLine 236: duration \u2014 test 2 '10:00' \u2192 600.0; test 3 missing \u2192 None. Both.\n\nLine 238-242: `if not info.get('title')` \u2014 True branch in tests 4,5; False branch in tests 2,3. Both covered.\nLine 239-242: first regex in test 4, second regex in test 5. Both covered.\n\nLine 231: `if lesson:` \u2014 True (2,3), False (4,5). Covered.\n\nLine 172-173: non-dict item. Covered.\nLine 179-182: section items. Covered (both chapter_number branches).\nLine 185-186: non-lesson items (quiz, empty dict). Covered.\nLine 187-189: lesson without url. Covered.\nLine 190-202: lessons with url. Covered.\nLine 204-210: return. Covered.\n\nLine 214-217: playlist regex + parse + [0]['file']. Covered.\nLine 218-221: _extract_m3u8_formats (mocked) + _sort_formats (real). Covered.\nLine 222-224: info dict. Covered.\nLine 226-230: lesson search \u2014 with match (2: window.lesson; 3: player.lesson) and without (4,5: default='{}'). Covered.\n\nGreat \u2014 full coverage of 155-243.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the `type_field` closure defined inside the loop (lines 175-176) \u2014 defined per iteration; covered.\n\nNow let me write the final test file.\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Tests for youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy.LinuxAcademyIE._real_extract.\"\"\"\n\nimport json\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import parse_duration, unified_timestamp\n\n\nclass _FakeDownloader(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal YoutubeDL stand-in: InfoExtractor only touches ``params``.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n\n\nCOURSE_URL = 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/1234'\nLESSON_URL = 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/1498/lesson/5993'\nLESSON_ITEM_ID = '1498-5993'\nCOURSE_ITEM_ID = '1234'\n\n\ndef _build_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, m3u8_formats=None):\n    \"\"\"Create a LinuxAcademyIE with all network access patched out.\"\"\"\n    captured = {}\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE(_FakeDownloader())\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(url_or_request, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        captured['download_url'] = url_or_request\n        captured['download_video_id'] = video_id\n        return webpage\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n\n    if m3u8_formats is not None:\n        def fake_extract_m3u8(m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n            captured['m3u8_url'] = m3u8_url\n            captured['m3u8_video_id'] = video_id\n            captured['m3u8_ext'] = ext\n            captured['m3u8_kwargs'] = kwargs\n            return m3u8_formats\n\n        monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract_m3u8)\n\n    return ie, captured\n\n\ndef _assert_timestamp(actual, date_str):\n    expected = unified_timestamp(date_str)\n    assert expected is not None, 'test date %r must be parseable' % date_str\n    assert actual == expected\n```\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 should I hardcode timestamps instead? Let me compute unified_timestamp('2019-06-25') = 1561420800 (if UTC). I could assert both: `assert entry['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-25') == 1561420800`. Hmm, if extract_timezone treats no-tz as UTC and uses calendar.timegm, then yes 1561420800. But if there's any local-timezone involvement, it'd differ. calendar.timegm is UTC-based, and strptime gives naive dt; `dt - timezone` with timezone=timedelta(0) \u2192 naive; timegm treats naive as UTC. So 1561420800. I'm fairly confident. But to be safe against my arithmetic, I'll assert equality with unified_timestamp(...) and additionally `is not None`. I could also hardcode... Let me include the hardcoded value too but... if wrong, test fails incorrectly. Risk. I'll skip hardcoding and just use the computed comparison + not-None guard. That's a solid postcondition check.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: the instruction says \"make VERY SURE that each test is correct\". Using unified_timestamp to compute the expected value is correct by construction (the extractor is specified to use unified_timestamp). The `is not None` guard ensures the date format is actually supported. Good.\n\nFor durations, parse_duration('5:00') = 300.0 \u2014 deterministic from the source shown: m = re.match('(?:(?:(?:(?P<days>[0-9]+):)?(?P<hours>[0-9]+):)?(?P<mins>[0-9]+):)?(?P<secs>[0-9]+)(?P<ms>\\.[0-9]+)?Z?$', '5:00') \u2192 mins='5', secs='00' \u2192 0 + 5*60 + 0 = 300.0. Yes. '6:30' \u2192 390.0. '1:00' \u2192 60.0. '10:00' \u2192 600.0. Hardcode these.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n```python\nclass TestCoursePath(object):\n    def test_course_playlist_full(self, monkeypatch):\n        module = {\n            'title': 'Linux Academy Course',\n            'md_desc': 'Course markdown description',\n            'desc': '<b>Course plain description</b>',\n            'duration': '1:00',\n            'items': [\n                'not-a-dict',\n                {},\n                {\n                    'type': {'name': 'Section', 'slug': 'section'},\n                    'course_name': 'Chapter One',\n                    'course_module': 'module-1',\n                },\n                {\n                    'type': {'name': 'Quiz', 'slug': 'quiz'},\n                },\n                {\n                    'type': {'name': 'lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n                    'url': None,\n                },\n                {\n                    'type': {'name': 'Lesson', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n                    'url': '/lesson/lesson-1',\n                    'title': 'Lesson One',\n                    'md_desc': 'Lesson one md description',\n                    'date': '2019-06-25',\n                    'duration': '5:00',\n                },\n                {\n                    'type': {'slug': 'lesson'},\n                    'url': 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2',\n                    'lesson_name': 'Lesson Two',\n                    'description': '<i>Lesson two description</i>',\n                    'created_on': '2019-06-26',\n                    'duration': '6:30',\n                },\n                {\n                    'type': {'name': 'section'},\n                    'course_name': 'Chapter Two',\n                    'course_module': 'module-2',\n                },\n                {\n                    'type': {'name': 'LESSON', 'slug': 'lesson'},\n                    'url': 'lesson/lesson-3',\n                    'title': None,\n                    'lesson_name': 'Lesson Three',\n                    'text': '<p>Lesson three text</p>',\n                    'duration': 'not-a-duration',\n                },\n            ],\n        }\n        webpage = '<script>window.module = %s;</script>' % json.dumps(module)\n        ie, captured = _build_ie(monkeypatch, webpage)\n\n        result = ie._real_extract(COURSE_URL)\n\n        # The right page was requested for the right item id.\n        assert captured['download_url'] == COURSE_URL\n        assert captured['download_video_id'] == COURSE_ITEM_ID\n\n        assert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\n        assert result['id'] == COURSE_ITEM_ID\n        assert result['title'] == 'Linux Academy Course'\n        assert result['description'] == 'Course markdown description'\n        assert result['duration'] == 60.0\n\n        entries = result['entries']\n        assert len(entries) == 3\n\n        first, second, third = entries\n\n        assert first['_type'] == 'url_transparent'\n        assert first['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key() == 'LinuxAcademy'\n        assert first['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/lesson-1'\n        assert first['title'] == 'Lesson One'\n        assert first['description'] == 'Lesson one md description'\n        _assert_timestamp(first['timestamp'], '2019-06-25')\n        assert first['duration'] == 300.0\n        assert first['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\n        assert first['chapter_id'] == 'module-1'\n        assert first['chapter_number'] == 1\n\n        assert second['url'] == 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2'\n        assert second['title'] == 'Lesson Two'\n        assert second['description'] == 'Lesson two description'\n        _assert_timestamp(second['timestamp'], '2019-06-26')\n        assert second['duration'] == 390.0\n        assert second['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\n        assert second['chapter_id'] == 'module-1'\n        assert second['chapter_number'] == 1\n\n        assert third['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/lesson/lesson-3'\n        assert third['title'] == 'Lesson Three'\n        assert third['description'] == 'Lesson three text'\n        assert third['timestamp'] is None\n        assert third['duration'] is None\n        assert third['chapter'] == 'Chapter Two'\n        assert third['chapter_id'] == 'module-2'\n        assert third['chapter_number'] == 2\n```\n\nWait \u2014 check urljoin for '/lesson/lesson-1' with base COURSE_URL = 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/1234': compat_urlparse.urljoin(base, '/lesson/lesson-1') \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/lesson-1'. Yes (leading slash \u2192 root-relative).\n\nFor 'lesson/lesson-3' (no leading slash): urljoin(base, 'lesson/lesson-3') \u2192 base path is '/cp/modules/view/id/1234' \u2192 resolves relative to '/cp/modules/view/id/' \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/lesson/lesson-3'. Yes.\n\nHmm wait, but first \u2014 the youtube_dl urljoin wrapper: `if re.match('^(?:[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9+-.]*:)?//', path): return path` \u2014 for '/lesson/lesson-1', no match (doesn't start with //). base matches '^(?:https?:)?//' \u2192 yes. Then compat_urlparse.urljoin(base, path). Good.\n\nFor 'https://example.com/lesson/lesson-2': matches `^(?:[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9+-.]*:)?//` \u2192 'https://' \u2192 returns path as-is. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one concern about the 'not-a-dict' string item and json.dumps: fine.\n\nAnother concern: the `{}` empty dict item \u2014 json.dumps({}) \u2192 '{}'. In the webpage: `...{\"type\": ...}...{}...` \u2014 hmm wait! The regex `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 non-greedy. The JSON is one big object; the match starts at the outer `{` and `.+?` expands until `}` followed by `\\s*;`. The only `}` followed by `;` is the final one. Inner `}` are followed by `,` or `}`. So full match. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtle catastrophic case: what if an inner value ends with `}` and then the rest of the JSON contains no `}` before... no, the regex needs `}\\s*;` \u2014 semicolon only at the very end. Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually \u2014 careful with `.+?` and the FIRST `}` followed by optional whitespace then `;`. In our JSON, the sequence `};` appears only at the very end. Good.\n\nNow the second course test:\n\n```python\n    def test_course_playlist_description_fallback_and_no_entries(self, monkeypatch):\n        module = {\n            'title': 'Empty Course',\n            'desc': '<b>Fallback course description</b>',\n            'items': [],\n        }\n        webpage = 'window.module = %s;' % json.dumps(module)\n        ie, captured = _build_ie(monkeypatch, webpage)\n\n        result = ie._real_extract(COURSE_URL)\n\n        assert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\n        assert result['id'] == COURSE_ITEM_ID\n        assert result['title'] == 'Empty Course'\n        assert result['description'] == 'Fallback course description'\n        assert result['duration'] is None\n        assert result['entries'] == []\n```\n\nWait \u2014 'duration' missing \u2192 parse_duration(None) \u2192 None (not isinstance str \u2192 None). Good.\n\nHmm \u2014 but does this test add coverage? Line 209's second branch (`clean_html(module.get('desc'))`). Yes. And empty entries. Good.\n\nShould these be in one class or standalone functions? Standalone functions are simpler. Let me use plain functions:\n\n```python\ndef test_course_playlist_full(monkeypatch): ...\ndef test_course_playlist_description_fallback(monkeypatch): ...\ndef test_single_video_with_window_lesson(monkeypatch): ...\ndef test_single_video_with_player_lesson_fallbacks(monkeypatch): ...\ndef test_single_video_title_from_lecture_heading(monkeypatch): ...\ndef test_single_video_title_from_lesson_name_variable(monkeypatch): ...\n```\n\nSingle video tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_with_window_lesson(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8\"}];'\n        'window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Intro Lesson\",'\n        ' \"md_desc\": \"Lesson md description\",'\n        ' \"date\": \"2019-06-25\", \"duration\": \"10:00\"};'\n    )\n    fake_formats = [{\n        'url': 'https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8',\n        'format_id': 'hls-1',\n        'ext': 'mp4',\n        'protocol': 'm3u8_native',\n    }]\n    ie, captured = _build_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, m3u8_formats=fake_formats)\n\n    result = ie._real_extract(LESSON_URL)\n\n    assert captured['download_url'] == LESSON_URL\n    assert captured['download_video_id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    assert captured['m3u8_url'] == 'https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8'\n    assert captured['m3u8_video_id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    assert captured['m3u8_ext'] == 'mp4'\n    assert captured['m3u8_kwargs'] == {'entry_protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'm3u8_id': 'hls'}\n\n    assert result['id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    assert result['formats'] == fake_formats  # hmm \u2014 _sort_formats may mutate (adds ext? already set)\n    assert result['title'] == 'Intro Lesson'\n    assert result['description'] == 'Lesson md description'\n    _assert_timestamp(result['timestamp'], '2019-06-25')\n    assert result['duration'] == 600.0\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `result['formats'] == fake_formats` \u2014 _sort_formats sorts in place and may add 'ext' (already present). The list object returned by the fake is the same object; sorting a single-element list doesn't change it. Equality holds. But to be safe, compare field-by-field:\n\n```python\nassert len(result['formats']) == 1\nfmt = result['formats'][0]\nassert fmt['url'] == 'https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8'\nassert fmt['format_id'] == 'hls-1'\n```\n\nSafer. \n\nHmm, wait \u2014 one issue with the webpage string: `'window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Intro Lesson\", \"md_desc\": ...};'` \u2014 the regex `window\\.lesson\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 the JSON contains no `};` inside. The JSON ends with `}` then `;`. Good. But note: the JSON contains `\", \"` etc. \u2014 fine.\n\nBut careful \u2014 the playlist regex: `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 `.+?` non-greedy: matches `[{...}]` up to first `]` followed by `;`. Our playlist JSON: `[{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8\"}]` \u2014 the first `]` is the final one. Good. But wait \u2014 the URL contains no `]`. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the webpage concatenates playlist part and lesson part into ONE line. The playlist regex `\\[.+?\\]` \u2014 could it match beyond? `.+?` non-greedy stops at first `]` followed by `\\s*;`. The first `]` in the string is the playlist's closing bracket, followed by `;`. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the lesson JSON contains no `[` or `]`. Good.\n\nAlso \u2014 the lesson regex `window\\.lesson\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 the lesson JSON's inner objects? None nested. The final `}` followed by `;`. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 `_parse_json` on the playlist capture: `self._parse_json(self._search_regex(...), item_id)[0]['file']` \u2014 the captured group is `[{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video.m3u8\"}]` \u2192 json.loads \u2192 list with one dict \u2192 [0]['file'] \u2192 the m3u8 URL. Good.\n\nTest 3:\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_with_player_lesson_fallbacks(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video2.m3u8\"}];'\n        'player.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"Second Lesson\",'\n        ' \"desc\": \"<b>Second lesson description</b>\",'\n        ' \"created_on\": \"2019-06-26\"};'\n    )\n    ...\n    result = ie._real_extract(LESSON_URL)\n    assert result['id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    assert result['title'] == 'Second Lesson'\n    assert result['description'] == 'Second lesson description'\n    _assert_timestamp(result['timestamp'], '2019-06-26')\n    assert result['duration'] is None\n```\n\nTest 4:\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_title_from_lecture_heading(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/video3.m3u8\"}];'\n        '<h3>Lecture: My Lecture Title</h3>'\n    )\n    ...\n    result = ie._real_extract(LESSON_URL)\n    assert result['id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    assert result['title'] == 'My Lecture Title'\n    assert 'description' not in result\n    assert 'timestamp' not in result\n    assert 'duration' not in result\n    assert len(result['formats']) == 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 the regex `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` on '<h3>Lecture: My Lecture Title</h3>': search finds '>Lecture